Periods and Exercise: Can You Work Out During Your Period?

Many people wonder whether it is safe to exercise during their period. In most cases, menstruation does not mean that you need to stop physical activity. Whether you exercise and how intensely you exercise should depend on how you feel.

Some people feel completely normal during their period and continue their usual workout routine. Others experience cramps, tiredness, headaches, or heavy bleeding and prefer lighter activities.

Walking is a simple option for people who want gentle movement. Stretching and light yoga may also feel comfortable for some people.

If you normally participate in strength training, running, cycling, or other forms of exercise, you may continue if you feel well enough. There is no universal requirement to avoid exercise during menstruation.

However, listening to your body is important. If you feel unusually weak, dizzy, or unwell, take a break and consider speaking with a healthcare professional if symptoms are significant.

Choosing a suitable menstrual product can also make exercise more comfortable. A pad that shifts during movement can be distracting, so proper positioning and a secure fit are important.

For heavier-flow days, you may prefer a longer or higher-absorbency sanitary pad. If you are exercising for an extended period, carry spare pads so you can change when necessary.

